::STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.1
Question:
A value calculated from a sample is called what?

{
=A. Statistic#Correct. A statistic is calculated from sample data.
~B. Parameter#A parameter describes an entire population.
~C. Population#A population is the full group, not the calculated value.
~D. Treatment#A treatment is assigned in an experiment.
}

::STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.2
Question:
Why is random sampling important?

{
~A. It guarantees no variation#Random samples still vary.
=B. It helps the sample represent the population#Correct. Random sampling reduces selection bias and supports representativeness.
~C. It replaces the need for a population#The population must still be defined.
~D. It always proves causation#Causation depends on study design, not random sampling alone.
}

::STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.3
Question:
A sample chosen because people are easy to reach is usually what type?

{
~A. Cluster sample#Cluster samples choose whole groups.
~B. Stratified sample#Stratified samples choose from subgroups.
=C. Convenience sample#Correct. Convenience samples use easily available participants.
~D. Census#A census includes everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.1
Question:
A survey estimate is 62% with margin of error 4 percentage points. What interval is represented?

{
~A. 54% to 58%#This interval is not centered at 62%.
~B. 62% to 66%#This only adds the margin.
~C. 4% to 62%#The margin is not the lower endpoint.
=D. 58% to 66%#Correct. Subtract and add 4 percentage points.
}

::STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.2
Question:
What does confidence level describe?

{
=A. The long-run success rate of the interval method#Correct. Confidence level describes the method over repeated samples.
~B. The number of people surveyed#Sample size is not the definition of confidence level.
~C. The exact percent who agree#A sample percent is an estimate, not confidence level.
~D. The graph title#A graph title does not define confidence.
}


